{"name":"Solstice Breeze","hex":"#cdd2bb","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"solstice","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["One of the two points in the ecliptic at which the sun is furthest from the celestial equator. This corresponds to one of two days in the year when the day is either longest or shortest."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/solstice"},{"term":"breeze","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A light, gentle wind.","Any activity that is easy, not testing or difficult."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To move casually, in a carefree manner.","To blow gently."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/breeze"}],"entries":[{"title":"Solstice","blocks":[{"text":"A solstice is the time when the Sun reaches its most northerly or southerly sun path relative to the celestial equator on the celestial sphere. Two solstices occur annually, around 20–22 June and 20–22 December. In many countries, the seasons of the year are defined by reference to the solstices and the equinoxes."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Solstice","wikidata":"Q123524"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ef/Solargraph_APEX.tif/lossy-page1-500px-Solargraph_APEX.tif.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ef/Solargraph_APEX.tif/lossy-page1-1280px-Solargraph_APEX.tif.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ef/Solargraph_APEX.tif/lossy-page1-500px-Solargraph_APEX.tif.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A solargraph taken from the Atacama Pathfinder Experiment at the Llano de Chajnantor Observatory in the southern hemisphere. This is a long-exposure photograph, with the image exposed for six months in a direction facing east of north, from mid-December 2009 until the southern winter solstice in June 2010.[15] The Sun's path each day can be seen from right to left in this image across the sky; the path of the following day runs slightly lower until the day of the winter solstice, whose path is the lowest one in the image.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASolargraph_APEX.tif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Length_of_solar_day.png/500px-Length_of_solar_day.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Length_of_solar_day.png/500px-Length_of_solar_day.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Derivative of −Δt, the so-called Equation of time. The axis on the right shows the length of the solar day, also called the synodic day.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALength_of_solar_day.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a3/Seasonearth.png/120px-Seasonearth.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a3/Seasonearth.png/250px-Seasonearth.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a3/Seasonearth.png/120px-Seasonearth.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASeasonearth.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/Earth-lighting-summer-solstice_EN.png/120px-Earth-lighting-summer-solstice_EN.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/Earth-lighting-summer-solstice_EN.png/250px-Earth-lighting-summer-solstice_EN.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wi